      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226477/assets/07b3922c7b14ed6bb5c613f81485fc4e155232fe0958805b9437c7ca1b4e608a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty $ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$\nthe graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2.\n$$",
